Dear Rachel,

Thank you again for taking the time to speak with me earlier =E2=80=94 I really a=
ppreciate your help with this.

As discussed, below is a short summary of the proposed expert instruction, =
which you are very welcome to circulate to potential experts on an initial,=20

no-obligation basis to assess interest and suitability and to invite CVs.

I have also attached the same summary as a short Word document, in case tha=
t is easier for you to circulate or forward internally.

Please do let me know if you would like anything refined for particular dis=
ciplines, or if any further context would be helpful at this stage.

Thank you again for your assistance.

Kind regards,
Nadia Zahmoul

Proposed Expert Instruction =E2=80=94 Summary (Initial Enquiry Only)

Context
The potential instruction arises from ongoing court proceedings and concern=
s the participation of an autistic individual within formal legal processes.

Nature of opinion sought
An independent, retrospective expert opinion within the expert=E2=80=99s professi=
onal field (for example psychology, disability participation, or neurodivers=
ity in institutional settings).

This is not a clinical diagnostic assessment and not a legal determination.

Materials for review
A structured, contemporaneous dataset consisting primarily of administrativ=
e correspondence and related metadata, covering approximately February=E2=80=93Jun=
e 2024.

The material documents how participation and communication issues were hand=
led over time in practice.

Indicative scope of opinion
From a professional perspective, the expert would be asked to consider:
how participation needs were addressed in practice over the relevant period=
,
whether any participation-related adjustments appear to have been implement=
ed, withdrawn, or applied inconsistently,
whether measures taken were likely to have been effective in practice, rath=
er than merely available in theory,
and the potential cumulative impact over time on the individual=E2=80=99s ability=
 to participate meaningfully.
What the expert would not be asked to do
Make findings of fact or legal conclusions
Comment on liability or fault
Provide advocacy or policy recommendations
Duplicate existing clinical diagnostic work (already completed separately)
Existing clinical context
The individual has a confirmed autism diagnosis, with clinical recommendati=
ons already provided by an NHS specialist service. These materials would be =
provided for background context only.

Expert profile sought
Psychologist, disability participation specialist, or relevant academic
Experience with autism and/or participation in formal, institutional, or de=
cision-making environments
Comfortable producing a clear, reasoned report for legal proceedings, if ul=
timately instructed
=20

=20

=20

=20

=20

=20


